==Trivia==
*The phrase 神の顔も三度まで ''Kami no Kao mo Sando Made'' (lit. "Even a God's Face is up to Three Times") in Cats of the Cosmos Chapter 3's Japanese title is a play on the idiom 仏の顔も三度まで ''Hotoke no Kao mo Sando Made'' ("Even the Buddha's Face is up to Three Times"), which is to say that even the most mild-mannered person will lose their temper if their patience is tested repeatedly.
*Many of the [[Enemy Bases|enemy bases]], [[Treasure]] names and stage appearances on the map in this saga are [[Pop Culture References|pop culture references]]. Some of the most noteworthy are stages [[Darararah (Cats of the Cosmos)|33]] through [[Ababwa (Cats of the Cosmos)|39]], all of which have different references to [[wikipedia:Mobile Suit Gundam|''Mobile Suit Gundam'']].
*The enemy bases of [[Jupiter (Cats of the Cosmos)|Jupiter]], [[Venus (Cats of the Cosmos)|Venus]] and [[Mercury (Cats of the Cosmos)|Mercury]] are visual puns on these planets' Japanese names:
**Jupiter's base is a tree because Jupiter's Japanese name, Mokusei (木星), means "Tree Star".
**Venus's base has golden sparkles because Venus's Japanese name, Kinsei (金星), means "Gold Star".
**Mercury's base is made of water because Mercury's Japanese name, Suisei (水星), means "Water Star".
*Most of the stage names and individual Treasure names are reused from Chapter 3 of [[The Battle Cats POP!]], Interstellar Invasion.
*Most [[anti-Starred Alien Treasures]] were named with a format "natural satellites, gemstone", e.g. "[[Phoebe Beryl]]" is named after [[wikipedia:Phoebe (moon)|Phoebe]], a moon of Saturn and [[wikipedia:Beryl#Golden beryl and heliodor|Beryl]], a gemstone that has a golden colored variant. The sole exception is [[Stellar Garnet]], in which Stellar means "star". The only thing connecting it to moons is that moons can be found by [[wikipedia:Occultation#Occultations by the Moon|stellar occultation]] (solar eclipse).

